WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:07.530
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Applications that

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:11.670
help us reduce the repetitive
tasks make us more productive,

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.190
but building them
is hard since it

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.160
stitches a lot of different
applications together.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.740
To explore one such
application, I've

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.010
invited David Ting, Senior
Vice President of Engineering

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.640
at Nylas, a company that's
revolutionizing the workflow

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.360
automation by collecting data
from multiple applications,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.850
compiling those insights,
and then creating

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.980
an end-to-end
automation workflow.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:35.760
Hi, David.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.870
Welcome to the show.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.040
DAVID TING: Hi, Priyanka.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.213
Thank you for having me.

00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:42.130
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I am
so excited to have you

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.220
on the show.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.860
Tell us a little bit
about you and Nylas.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.040
DAVID TING: I am the
SVP of Engineering,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.830
and I'm responsible
for Nylas's product

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.610
and technical execution.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.600
Nylas's mission is to make
the world more productive,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.140
and we built up a very
scalable and secure platform

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.260
to handle communication data
driving business process

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.100
automation.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.310
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, so
how does it matter to me,

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.892
you, as end users?

00:01:07.892 --> 00:01:09.850
DAVID TING: If there are
enough developers that

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.100
write all the
applications you need

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.310
to automate and
streamlined workflow,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.330
the world would be
much different today.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.940
Why do I have to enter
receipts for expenses?

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.120
Why is it so hard to juggle
my calendar when I just

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.420
need two hours of focus time?

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.180
Building applications to
reduce repetitive tasks

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.385
are really hard,
and Nylas's mission

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:33.540
is to make it easier for you
to build high-RI application

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.080
and make you a
star in your work.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.580
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Wow, productivity

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.770
sounds really cool,
and yes, I do not

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.250
want to upload my
receipts into a system.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.530
It should just
happen automatically.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.610
How does this actually work?

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.550
DAVID TING: So we
capture real-time data

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:53.410
from service providers such
as Gmail, Microsoft, IMAP.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.750
We have data pipelines
power ML models

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.600
behind the scene to create
intelligent data objects.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.170
Data from these insights
trigger programmable workflows.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.240
And the challenge is
building up a distributed

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.490
real-time architecture for
the billions of messages

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.680
that we receive each day and
keep the end-to-end latency

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.200
low and predictable.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.700
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
All right, so now

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.820
that we have put the customer
expectation in perspective,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.880
and now that we know how
Nylas gathers the data

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.380
and creates these insights,
what are the technical drivers

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.088
for working with Google Cloud?

00:02:25.088 --> 00:02:27.130
DAVID TING: We came to
Google with what we viewed

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.140
as a very challenging mission.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.210
We have a large
enterprise customer

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.570
in the financial services
space with very strict security

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:35.650
requirements.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.530
PII can not be
stored persistently,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.150
and the lifetime of
each server needs

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.510
to be short to avoid
potential in-memory hacks.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.270
This was a great reason
for us to rethink the Nylas

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.530
architecture.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.530
The legacy Nylas architecture
was built with Python flasks

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:51.635
on top of AWS.

00:02:51.635 --> 00:02:54.295
It was difficult and
very costly to scale,

00:02:54.295 --> 00:02:57.618
and the end-to-end
performance is unreliable.

00:02:57.618 --> 00:02:58.910
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Interesting.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.400
Why was it so costly to scale?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.690
DAVID TING: Nylas processes over
20 terabytes of data every day,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.490
and the high volume
of read and writes

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.700
are mainly hitting against
hundreds of MySQL shards.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.950
The workload is partitioned
as email accounts

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.470
across a farm with
a static assignment.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:18.880
Because volume of the emails is
unpredictable for each account,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.850
there are many hotspots that
emerge throughout the day,

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.140
causing high variability in
latency and, in severe cases,

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.730
reliability issues.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.700
In order to address this, we
had to overprovision the system

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.060
by a good margin in order to
keep that latency in control.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.480
Based on extensive load
testing, with the same traffic

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.090
load, whenever we double the
infrastructure footprint,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.390
we can lower the
P90 latency in half.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.210
And at our current scale,
doubling our footprint,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.570
it really means a million
dollars in cloud cost,

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.940
so that's not a
sustainable strategy.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.150
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow,
that is a lot of money.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.910
All right, so three
big major issues

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.740
that you set out to solve--

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.450
the security, scalability,
performance, and, obviously,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.420
cost.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.170
So why did you
pick Google Cloud?

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.670
DAVID TING: In my
mind, Google Cloud

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.650
has the best
distributed technology

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.040
and has been a true engineering
partner throughout the journey.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.100
The security, scalability,
and stability of the platform

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.450
is second to none.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.840
GKE is currently the best
option out of any cloud provider

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.940
to run Kubernetes workload
securely at scale,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.290
and one of the most performant
data stores in Spanner.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.420
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So now I want
to get into my favorite part,

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.030
which is the architecture.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.760
How did you approach
it, what was it like,

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.057
and how is it going?

00:04:38.057 --> 00:04:39.640
DAVID TING: We were
able to transition

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.040
to a brand new
architecture and achieve

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.320
a huge ROI for the project.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.980
The big bets of using GKE
to handle the container

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.800
orchestration, Pub/Sub
for message bus,

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.220
and Spanner for relational data
store has paid off handsomely.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.310
We went live on June
4, and we really

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.450
knocked it out of the park.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.040
Everything has been
scaling effectively.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.700
For example, when
an application has

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.600
performed over 1 billion
transactions in less than a

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.260
month--

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.510
and then, this is
on top of Spanner--

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.780
what I cannot believe is
that the latency is actually

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.050
controlled very well,
to 7 milliseconds.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.480
And this really speaks
volumes to the robustness

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.400
of GCP services.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.360
By working closely with
the GCP Account Team

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.220
and a great partner in Bespin.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.280
We're able to address most of
the issues before the launch.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.530
So our customer's
QA team went in

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.260
and tested our
initial prototype.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.300
They found zero issues.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.330
And we spent the rest of the
time optimizing the workload

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:41.418
as we scale on GCP.

00:05:41.418 --> 00:05:42.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Great!

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.250
Well, how did you go
from-- you mentioned

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.910
that you went from a
Python-based application to Go.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.280
You did an entire rewrite.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.810
How did you do that?

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:57.850
And moving from virtual
machine instances on EC2

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:01.630
to GKE, how did
that process happen?

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:05.020
DAVID TING: As part of the
re-imagination exercise,

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.580
we had to take some big bets.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.490
And what we did was
we actually decided

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.700
to rewrite our services
in Go, because we

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.390
believe that there's a 10x
throughput improvement when

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:17.320
we do this.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.840
The second part is
moving, basically,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:26.950
the orchestration here from
Cloud-specific and an AWS

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:28.060
autoscaler group.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.160
We basically want to
go with the industry

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.250
standard in GKE or Kubernetes.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:36.070
So we used GKE to
orchestrate the containers,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.050
and do to the
security requirements,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.310
the nodes have to be
cycled very quickly.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.190
So we're able to take
advantage of the fact

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:46.660
that we can have up to 15,000
nodes in a Kubernetes cluster

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:47.380
on GKE.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.870
And that's actually something
that other cloud providers

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:50.920
don't provide.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:53.290
In addition, we're
also using gVisor

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.080
to run the containers to
create a strong isolation

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.760
between the application
and the operating system.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:02.230
This helps us to lock down the
host memory and storage access

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.780
and enforce least permission
principle at the operating

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.890
system level.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.530
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Earlier,
you mentioned 20-plus terabytes

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.280
of data being processed in
hundreds of MySQL shards--

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.010
which, obviously,
wasn't really efficient

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.650
and was also extremely costly.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:21.490
How did you solve that as you
re-imagined this architecture

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:22.690
in Google Cloud?

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.310
DAVID TING: We
made some big bets.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.790
And so we used Spanner as
the relational data store

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.190
to keep states for
the application

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.330
and also store the keys for
each one of the accounts

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:35.690
we need to connect to.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:38.615
So fast read and write
access performance

00:07:38.615 --> 00:07:41.590
is extremely critical for
a large number of calls.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.790
We need the consistent
high performance

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:47.000
to have predictable
end-to-end latency.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.980
And that actually has
paid off by betting on--

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:54.820
running on GCP services
versus doing it ourselves.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.730
Lastly, Nylas does
not have to hold PII.

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:02.290
So this information is pushed so
that once our customers receive

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:06.520
it, we purge the information
from our store for security

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:07.390
reasons.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:08.890
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
For communication

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:12.550
between those microservices,
you used Pub/Sub.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.490
Can we talk a little
bit about why and how

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.440
Pub/Sub has been helpful?

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.370
DAVID TING: Yes, so we went from
a very database-centric design

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.050
to an event-centric design.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:26.350
So for us, the message bus
is the key and the heart

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.390
of our whole design.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:33.340
So we chose Google Pub/Sub for
an event-driven microservices

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:34.600
architecture.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:39.070
And just as a comparison, in
the AWS world and the legacy

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.780
architecture,
because it's actually

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.870
more of a data-driven
architecture,

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:47.350
the data is written into Dynamo.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:50.380
Then, it gets converted
by a lambda function,

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:53.570
and then it goes to
Kinesis events stream.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:56.290
So in Google Cloud, we
want to simplify this.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:57.970
So we want it event first.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.240
And so we rewrote it
directly on Google Pub/Sub.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.740
So it actually puts a lot
of strain on the system,

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:07.120
so there's a lot of trust that
we have in how this scales

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.800
and how it's going to perform
reliably and consistently.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.190
And we were very happy with the
experience on Google Pub/Sub,

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.320
where the performance
latency is minimal,

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.540
even when we have to
burst to tens of thousands

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.050
of writes per second.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.860
So you can even
think about Event Bus

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.810
to basically be like
a data store for us,

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:30.040
because it is that reliable
and scalable for us.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.260
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah,
that is amazing to hear.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.530
What are some of the
benefits that you've

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:39.490
achieved after migrating
to Google Cloud?

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.210
DAVID TING: This cannot happen
without exceptional service,

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.400
so we're very happy with
everyone we've worked with

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:46.840
at GCP.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.530
We were able to get over 30
times the throughput per node

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.210
after the rewrite in
Go, and in addition,

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:56.240
the elasticity of the
architecture has been amazing.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.970
It's reliable.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.530
Latency doesn't go down.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.560
Performance is actually
stable as we actually

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.180
have traffic spike.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:06.940
It's just outright
amazing when you

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.250
work with the
high-scale architecture.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:11.590
Our customer has been
migrating their entire user

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.230
base onto Nylas in
June, and the process

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.850
has been extremely smooth.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.310
And in addition, during
Amazon Prime Day,

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:21.640
where just a lot of bursts
in e-commerce transaction

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:25.360
come in within the minute,
we saw that a number of nodes

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.730
went from a handful
to tens of thousands

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.930
of nodes within a few minutes.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.070
And we're able to handle
the peak consistently,

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.800
and the P90 performance
actually was stable,

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.990
even with those spikes.

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.460
And it's just really
amazing to think back,

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:42.380
and we were able to
accomplish out all

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:43.940
of that in just three months.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.910
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow, three
months is a pretty impressive

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:50.660
timeline for a migration this
big where you had to rewrite

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:57.050
from one language to another--
in this case, Python to Go--

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.560
and then also learning
all these new tools--

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:04.580
GKE, Spanner, and Pub/Sub.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:08.280
And I'm sure it was a
very massive team effort.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.130
What was your
experience like working

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:13.568
with the Google team and
the partner in this process?

00:11:13.568 --> 00:11:15.860
DAVID TING: I want to first
thank the Nylas engineering

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:16.400
team.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.200
I have not worked
with a group that

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.350
is as talented and as dedicated
in their work in my career.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.830
Secondly, I would like to thank
our partner, GCP and Bespin

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.480
for making this happen.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:29.780
We're able to work as one
team over emails in Slack

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.090
and resolve issues in real time.

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.770
Most companies would
need at least one

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:35.840
year to pull off an
architectural overhaul

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.477
of this order of magnitude.

00:11:37.477 --> 00:11:39.560
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: This
is absolutely phenomenal--

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:42.660
the speed, the collaboration.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.580
What is the road ahead?

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.600
What does future
technical enhancements

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.030
look like for your architecture?

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:52.520
DAVID TING: With the
success we experienced,

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.230
we want to shoot bigger.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:58.310
So we had the confidence to
handle more challenges ahead

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.830
and we're looking
to work with GCP

